  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "What is the remainder when $-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+1$ is divided by $2{x}^{2}-x+5?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+1=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+0x+1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}-x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+0x+1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+0x+1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-x+5}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-{x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $x-4.$",
